## Overview of the path

`POST /api/owners` → Spring Security filter chain → `OwnersApi.addOwner` (mapping + `@Valid` bean validation) → `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (`@PreAuthorize` + manual blank-check) → `OwnerMapper.toOwner` → `ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner` (`@Transactional`) → `OwnerRepository.save` → JPA `persist`. Validation errors and the custom missing-fields exception are handled by `ExceptionControllerAdvice`.

Note: two owner controllers exist (`v1`, `v2`) but only V1 implements `addOwner`; V2 only exposes a paged list. The active profile is `spring-data-jpa` (both `src/main` and `src/test` `application.properties`), so the persistence sink is the Spring Data repository, not the `Jpa*Impl` class.

---

## 1. Mapping + controller method

The mapping annotations live on the OpenAPI-generated interface, not the controller.

`/home/daniel/sandbox/target/generated-sources/openapi/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/api/OwnersApi.java` (lines 52, 79-87):

```java
String PATH_ADD_OWNER = "/owners";
...
@RequestMapping(
    method = RequestMethod.POST,
    value = OwnersApi.PATH_ADD_OWNER,          // "/owners"
    produces = { "application/json" },
    consumes = { "application/json" }
)
default ResponseEntity<OwnerDto> addOwner(
    @Parameter(name = "OwnerFieldsDto", description = "The pet owner", required = true) @Valid @RequestBody OwnerFieldsDto ownerFieldsDto
) { ... default 501 body ... }
```

The interface is annotated `@Validated` (line 44), and `@Valid` on the body (line 86) is what triggers Bean Validation on `OwnerFieldsDto`. The class-level `@RequestMapping("/api")` on the controller combines with `/owners` to yield `/api/owners`.

The concrete handler — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java` (class annotations lines 53-56; method lines 101-112):

```java
@RestController
@CrossOrigin(exposedHeaders = "errors, content-type")
@RequestMapping("/api")
public class OwnerRestControllerV1 implements OwnersApi {
    ...
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole(@roles.OWNER_ADMIN)")
    @Override
    public ResponseEntity<OwnerDto> addOwner(OwnerFieldsDto ownerFieldsDto) {
        rejectMissingOrBlankFields(ownerFieldsDto);
        HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
        Owner owner = ownerMapper.toOwner(ownerFieldsDto);
        this.clinicService.saveOwner(owner);
        OwnerDto ownerDto = ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner);
        headers.setLocation(UriComponentsBuilder.newInstance()
            .path("/api/owners/{id}").buildAndExpand(owner.getId()).toUri());
        return new ResponseEntity<>(ownerDto, headers, HttpStatus.CREATED);
    }
```

The manual second-stage validation, same file (lines 215-239):

```java
private void rejectMissingOrBlankFields(OwnerFieldsDto fields) {
    List<String> missing = new ArrayList<>();
    if (isBlank(fields.getFirstName()))  missing.add("firstName");
    if (isBlank(fields.getLastName()))   missing.add("lastName");
    if (isBlank(fields.getAddress()))    missing.add("address");
    if (isBlank(fields.getCity()))       missing.add("city");
    if (isBlank(fields.getTelephone()))  missing.add("telephone");
    if (!missing.isEmpty()) {
        throw new RequiredFieldsMissingException(missing);
    }
}
private boolean isBlank(String value) {
    return value == null || value.isBlank();
}
```

Order of validation: (1) `@Valid` bean validation on the DTO runs before the handler body; if it fails, the handler never executes. (2) `rejectMissingOrBlankFields` runs first inside the handler (catches whitespace-only values that pass `@Size(min=1)`), before mapping/persisting.

---

## 2. Request DTO with all Bean Validation annotations

`/home/daniel/sandbox/target/generated-sources/openapi/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/dto/OwnerFieldsDto.java`. Constraints are on the getters:

- `firstName` (line 62): `@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "^[\\p{L}]+([ '-][\\p{L}]+){0,2}$") @Size(min = 1, max = 30)`
- `lastName` (line 83): `@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "^[\\p{L}]+([ '-][\\p{L}]+){0,2}\\.?$") @Size(min = 1, max = 30)`
- `address` (line 104): `@NotNull @Size(min = 1, max = 255)`
- `city` (line 125): `@NotNull @Size(min = 1, max = 80)`
- `telephone` (line 146): `@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "^[0-9]*$") @Size(min = 1, max = 20)`

Note the DTO `telephone` pattern is `^[0-9]*$` (any length of digits, up to 20). This differs from the entity's stricter `^[0-9]{10}$` (see section 6).

---

## 3. Custom validator classes

None. All validation uses standard Jakarta Bean Validation annotations (`@NotNull`, `@Size`, `@Pattern`, `@NotEmpty`, `@Digits`). The only "custom" validation is the imperative `rejectMissingOrBlankFields` method above plus the `RequiredFieldsMissingException` at `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/RequiredFieldsMissingException.java` (a `RuntimeException` carrying the offending field names, lines 25-37).

---

## 4. Service-layer method

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicServiceImpl.java` (lines 232-237):

```java
@Override
@Transactional
public void saveOwner(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException {
    ownerRepository.save(owner);
}
```

`OwnerMapper.toOwnerDto` is also called after save (for the response) — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`. The mapping from request to entity (line 25-27) explicitly ignores `id` and `pets`:

```java
@Mapping(target = "id", ignore = true)
@Mapping(target = "pets", ignore = true)
Owner toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto ownerDto);
```

---

## 5. Repository / persistence calls

`OwnerRepository.save` — interface at `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/OwnerRepository.java` (line 66).

Active profile is `spring-data-jpa`, so the implementation is Spring Data's generated proxy for `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/springdatajpa/SpringDataOwnerRepository.java` (`@Profile("spring-data-jpa")`, extends `Repository<Owner, Integer>` — `save` inherited/generated by Spring Data, ends in `EntityManager.persist`).

The alternative `@Profile("jpa")` impl at `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/jpa/JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl.java` (lines 92-100) shows the insert/update decision explicitly:

```java
@Override
public void save(Owner owner) {
    if (owner.getId() == null) {
        this.em.persist(owner);
    } else {
        this.em.merge(owner);
    }
}
```

Since the mapper ignores `id`, a newly created owner has `id == null` → INSERT via `persist`. There is also a JDBC impl (`repository/jdbc/JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl.java`) used only under the `jdbc` profile.

---

## 6. Entity lifecycle callbacks

None. A grep for `@PrePersist`/`@PostPersist`/`@PreUpdate`/`@PostLoad` across the codebase returned nothing.

Entity hierarchy: `Owner extends Person extends BaseEntity`.

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` — `@Entity @Table(name="owners")`. Field constraints (lines 38-50): `address` `@NotEmpty`; `city` `@NotEmpty`; `telephone` `@NotEmpty @Digits(fraction=0, integer=10) @Pattern(regexp="^[0-9]{10}$", message="Phone number must be exactly 10 digits")`. `pets` is `@OneToMany(cascade=ALL, mappedBy="owner", fetch=EAGER)`.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Person.java` — `@MappedSuperclass`; `firstName` and `lastName` both `@NotEmpty` (lines 31-37).
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/BaseEntity.java` — `@MappedSuperclass`; `id` is `@Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)` (lines 33-35), so the DB assigns the id on insert.

These entity-level constraints matter: even though the mapper copies DTO fields to the entity, if Hibernate/Bean Validation runs on flush (`ddl` + `jakarta.persistence` validation on pre-persist), the entity's stricter `telephone` `^[0-9]{10}$` applies. The DTO allowed any-length digit string, so a non-10-digit phone can pass DTO validation but could fail at the entity level.

---

## 7. Events / logging / email / other side-effects

No application events published, no email sending, no messaging along this path. The only logging is in the exception advice (`logger.error`/`warn`/`debug`) when an error occurs (section 9). The only "side effect" on the success path is setting the `Location` response header. No caching annotations are triggered on `saveOwner` (the class comment mentions `@Cacheable` as a placeholder, but `saveOwner`/`save` are not cached).

---

## 8. Security / @PreAuthorize / filters

Method guard: `@PreAuthorize("hasRole(@roles.OWNER_ADMIN)")` on `addOwner`. `@roles` resolves to the Spring bean `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/security/Roles.java`, where `OWNER_ADMIN = "ROLE_OWNER_ADMIN"` (line 8). `hasRole` requires authority `ROLE_OWNER_ADMIN`.

Whether the guard is actually enforced depends on profile, via two mutually exclusive configs keyed on `petclinic.security.enable`:

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/security/BasicAuthenticationConfig.java` — active when `petclinic.security.enable=true`. Annotated `@EnableMethodSecurity(prePostEnabled=true)` (this is what makes `@PreAuthorize` effective), CSRF disabled, `anyRequest().authenticated()`, HTTP Basic, JDBC-backed users/roles.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/security/DisableSecurityConfig.java` — active when `petclinic.security.enable=false`. `anyRequest().permitAll()`, and crucially does NOT enable method security, so `@PreAuthorize` is not evaluated.

Config values: `src/main/resources/application.properties` sets `petclinic.security.enable=false` (line 49) → security effectively OFF for normal runs. `src/test/resources/application.properties` sets it to `true` (line 43) → security and `@PreAuthorize` enforced under test.

The advice also re-throws `AccessDeniedException` (see below) so a role failure surfaces as 403, not 500.

---

## 9. Global exception handlers / @ControllerAdvice

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java` (`@ControllerAdvice`). Relevant handlers:

- `handleMethodArgumentNotValidException` (lines 134-170) — catches `@Valid` failures on the DTO, returns 400 with a `ProblemDetail`, an `errors` array of distinct invalid field names, and a `schemaValidationErrors` list formatted as `Field '<field>' <message> (rejected value: <value>)`.
- `handleRequiredFieldsMissingException` (lines 181-192) — catches the custom exception from `rejectMissingOrBlankFields`, returns 400 with `errors` = the missing field names.
- `handleAccessDeniedException` (lines 85-88) — re-throws so Spring Security produces 403 (or the entry point for anonymous), preventing the general handler from turning it into a 500.
- `handleDataIntegrityViolationException` (lines 114-125) — maps DB constraint violations to 404.
- `handleGeneralException` (lines 97-104) — catch-all → 500.

---

## 10. @Transactional annotations on the path

- Service: `ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner` is `@Transactional` (read-write) — line 233, using `org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional`. This is the transaction boundary that wraps the `persist`.
- Controller: `addOwner` is NOT `@Transactional` (only `deleteOwner` carries `jakarta.transaction.Transactional` on the controller, line 131 — not on the create path).
- Repository: no explicit `@Transactional`; runs within the service transaction (Spring Data's `save` has its own default transactional semantics but joins the existing one).

So the single transaction for create is opened at `ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner` and commits after the method returns; the mapping to `OwnerDto` and `Location` header happen back in the controller after commit.

